content.explanation:
A unit vector is a vector whose magnitude is exactly 1 - nothing more, nothing less. Its entire job is to point in a direction. We usually write it with a little hat on top, like v̂, to remind ourselves "this one's just direction, size is locked at 1."

**How Do You Make One?**
Take any vector v and divide it by its own magnitude: {{viz:formula-01}}

v̂ = v / |v|

That's it. You're not changing the direction at all - you're just rescaling the length down to exactly 1. This process is called **normalizing** the vector. {{viz:anim-01}}

**Quick Example**
Say v = (3, 4), so |v| = 5. Divide each component by 5: v̂ = (3/5, 4/5) = (0.6, 0.8). Check the magnitude of that: √(0.6² + 0.8²) = √(0.36+0.64) = √1 = 1. Perfect - still length 1, still pointing the same way as the original. {{viz:chart-01}}

**The Famous î, ĵ, k̂**
You'll constantly run into three special unit vectors: î (points along x), ĵ (points along y), and k̂ (points along z). They're the building blocks - any vector can be written as a combination of them, like v = 3î + 4ĵ. Think of them as the standard "east" and "north" arrows that every other vector gets built from.

**Why Bother?**
Unit vectors let us cleanly separate "how big" from "which way." Anytime you only care about direction - like figuring out which way friction pushes, or which way a normal force points - you reach for a unit vector. {{viz:text-01}} They also make writing vector equations way tidier, since you can scale a direction by whatever magnitude you need. {{viz:simulation-01}}
